  "preferences": {
    "travel_and_vacations": "Travel for Grace is often organized around a live event: women's basketball, women's soccer, or a band she particularly wants to see. The event gives a trip a point, but she does not want the rest of the days filled with scheduled activities. She likes being in a crowd that is genuinely invested in what is happening, noticing the local rituals around a game, and having a couple of good meals without needing a famous restaurant reservation. She is not especially motivated by collecting landmarks or visiting as many places as possible. A trip can be worthwhile even if most of it is spent in one city and she comes home knowing only a few neighborhoods.\n\nShe usually travels with her longtime friend Dana, who follows women's basketball too, or with her younger brother when their schedules line up. She is fine going to an event alone if nobody else can make it, though she enjoys having someone to talk through the game with afterward. For lodging she prefers a clean private room with a convenient route to the venue, not an elaborate resort. She will spend extra for a well-timed flight or a hotel that saves a difficult late-night journey, but not for an upgrade whose main appeal is prestige. She uses transit when practical, and rents a car when the destination or timing makes that easier. She likes a loose plan with the major tickets and lodging handled in advance; meals, a bookstore stop, or a free afternoon can stay undecided.\n\nHer rotating on-call schedule limits how far ahead she can promise dates, though the four-day workweek gives her some useful long weekends. In a typical year she takes one trip of five to seven nights and several shorter trips, often to Seattle or Portland for games, concerts, or friends. Family visits also use some weekends and leave. She does not assume that a high gross income makes every trip easy: rent, retirement contributions, car upkeep, and occasional help for her mother all compete with travel savings.\n\nIn July 2025, Grace went with Dana to Indianapolis for WNBA All-Star weekend. They stayed four nights in a basic hotel within a reasonable trip of the arena and booked the main travel after the event dates were confirmed. Grace was most excited for the game and the public fan events, where she enjoyed the energy and the mix of longtime fans and people who had come with friends. Dana wanted to pay for a premium add-on and squeeze in more scheduled activities; Grace preferred to keep the hotel simple and have a free afternoon to recover from the crowds. They skipped the add-on, ate at casual local places, and spent their last morning lingering over breakfast rather than rushing to another attraction. Grace came home hoarse and pleased, with a little regret that the trip was so short. She had used several leave days and chose not to extend it, saving time for a family commitment later in the year.\n\nFor a future longer trip, she is curious about seeing women's football in England and Scotland, perhaps with a few days in each place rather than a packed national tour. That idea is still a wish, not a booking. She also enjoys simple Washington and Oregon weekends: a concert in another city, a ferry ride, or staying with friends. She is less interested in vacations that require constant driving or strenuous outdoor activity than in an event that gives the days a shared focus.",
    "reading": "Grace reads for pleasure several times a week, most often before bed or while eating breakfast on a day off. She likes suspense novels, investigative nonfiction, and books about labor history or the workings of cities. She borrows ebooks and audiobooks through the library and buys a paperback now and then to take on a trip. If a book does not hold her attention, she switches to something else without feeling she has to finish it.",
    "other_interests": "She follows the Seattle Storm and Portland's women's soccer scene, watches games at home or with friends, and goes to live music when a show appeals. She has a small collection of concert posters and keeps ticket stubs, but does not try to own merchandise from every event. She enjoys a casual dinner after a game, neighborhood coffee shops, and occasional walks along the waterfront. Her weeknight cooking is usually practical—rice, vegetables, eggs, or leftovers—rather than an elaborate hobby."
