  "preferences": {
    "travel_and_vacations": "Grace likes travel that gives her a sustained reason to pay attention rather than a packed sequence of sights. A hands-on class, a small museum collection, or an unfamiliar working craft can give a trip its shape; she enjoys seeing how a place makes and uses things. She is not looking for a strenuous outdoor challenge or a luxury resort. She prefers one base for several nights, a private and comfortable room, and enough unscheduled time to walk, read, or decide on dinner late in the day. A workshop gives her some companionship and conversation without requiring her to coordinate every hour with a travel partner. She is comfortable traveling alone, though she enjoys sharing a meal or a side outing with a friend when schedules happen to align.\n\nAs a small-business owner, she cannot simply use a standard bank of paid vacation days. She usually blocks about two weeks away in the quieter part of the year, with a few shorter breaks when her staff can cover the shop. She plans well ahead around orders and staffing, but does not plan each day of a trip in detail. She will spend extra for a class she genuinely wants or lodging that makes it easy to reach the place she is visiting; she is less interested in a costly room upgrade that mainly signals luxury. She generally flies for distant trips and rents a car only when the destination makes it worthwhile. She does not try to visit every nearby town just because she has traveled a long way.\n\nIn October 2025, she spent nine nights in Santa Fe, traveling on her own for a five-day natural-dye and paper workshop. She chose the workshop over a shorter class plus a rushed side trip to Taos, and booked a simple private room close enough to get to class on foot. The days were structured and social; afterward she liked being able to eat alone, browse, or rest without negotiating a plan. She spent one free day looking at local art and another mostly reading and walking. She came home with stained hands, a few samples, and a feeling that she had learned more from staying put than she would have from adding another destination. She did briefly wonder whether she should have seen more of northern New Mexico, but was glad she had paid for the course rather than a more elaborate itinerary. Her next trip might be another workshop in a different medium, but she has not booked one.\n\nShorter vacations are usually low-key: a couple of nights on the Oregon coast, a visit to a friend in the Bay Area, or an overnight somewhere with a good bookstore and a quiet place to walk. She sometimes travels with a longtime college friend, especially for a weekend, but their schedules rarely allow a long shared trip. She is content with that; she would rather travel alone than turn a vacation into a calendar negotiation.",
    "reading": "She reads for pleasure several evenings a week, often after dinner. She likes novels with sharp observation, essays, and books about art, design, or the history of ordinary objects. She uses the library for fiction and buys a book when she wants to keep referring to it. A book is allowed to be merely enjoyable; she does not insist on finishing one that has started to feel like work.",
    "other_interests": "Outside the shop, Grace visits small art exhibitions, takes long walks, and occasionally attends a printmaking or book-arts event. She likes cooking simple meals and trying a new recipe when she has a free weekend, but does not make elaborate cooking a daily project. She enjoys quiet cafes and independent shops more than nightlife."
