  "preferences": {
    "travel_and_vacations": "Travel is a way for Grace to spend sustained attention on a place, especially its wildlife and the changing seasons. She is an enthusiastic amateur birder, not a competitive lister: she enjoys learning calls, watching how birds behave, and noticing what is present at a particular time of year. A productive morning outdoors followed by a relaxed lunch and a quiet evening suits her better than a day packed with attractions. She likes having a local guide for a habitat or boat outing where their knowledge makes a real difference, but does not want a guide or group to dictate every hour. She is happy with a simple private room or a comfortable small motel, and appreciates a good bed and easy access to the day's activity more than a fancy property. She is not drawn to camping as the default, and she will rent a car when a trip depends on reaching wildlife areas at dawn. A city stop can be pleasant, especially for a bookstore or a good meal, but she does not feel compelled to add one just to make an itinerary look complete.\n\nGrace usually takes one trip of about a week each year, with occasional long weekends to Oregon or Washington wetlands. Her work income gives her room to travel, but the mortgage, retirement savings, and car expenses mean she chooses trips deliberately. She has around three weeks of paid leave and generally uses some for family visits or home projects. She starts with the season and the main place she wants to visit, then books flights, lodging, and any essential guided outing; she leaves many meals and shorter walks open. She most often travels with Nisha, a friend from her community-college days who shares her interest in birds but is more eager to fit in extra stops. Grace is comfortable taking a short solo trip too, particularly if coordinating calendars would be more trouble than the company is worth.\n\nIn February 2026, Grace and Nisha spent seven nights on the Texas coast, flying to Corpus Christi and renting a car. They stayed in Rockport and visited Aransas National Wildlife Refuge, with one guided boat outing to learn more about the local habitat and wildlife. Nisha wanted to add a couple of nights in San Antonio on the way back. Grace argued for staying near the coast instead: the extra mornings would let them return to the refuge at different times rather than spend another day packing and driving. They skipped San Antonio and had two slower mornings, including one that was foggy and not especially productive for sightings. Grace still felt the choice was right; she liked the unhurried rhythm and the guide's explanations, though she understood why Nisha wanted more variety. She returned rested, with several pages of notes in her pocket notebook, and did not immediately start planning the next trip.\n\nAt home, she takes her binoculars to nearby wetlands when weather and work allow, often for a couple of hours rather than an all-day outing. For a future longer vacation she is curious about birding in Portugal, but she would want to make it a real holiday too, with time for local food and a few days without an early alarm. She does not assume that every trip needs to be international or ambitious; an overnight near the coast can be enough when she has only a weekend.",
    "reading": "Grace reads for pleasure several evenings a week and often brings a paperback on a trip. She likes contemporary fiction, narrative nonfiction about science or history, and accessible books on birds and habitats. She uses the library and buys books she expects to revisit. She sometimes reads several books at once and does not force herself to finish one that has become a chore.",
    "food_and_free_time": "She enjoys Korean dishes she grew up eating as well as casual restaurants with a strong local following. At home, she cooks simple dinners on weeknights and is more willing to experiment on a Sunday. She likes a quiet coffee shop, a used-book store, and a walk where she can stop to look at something without making the outing an exercise goal. She has no interest in turning every social plan into a reservation or a special occasion.",
    "transportation": "She drives for work and for trips to refuges or coast towns, but prefers not to drive into unfamiliar city centers when transit or walking is practical."
  },
  "beliefs": "Grace describes herself as libertarian. She places a high value on individual choice, privacy, freedom of expression, and the ability of small businesses to make decisions without needless government restrictions. Her work has made her wary of rules that are vague or burdensome, though it has also shown her that contracts and clear accountability matter when people face real losses. She is not a party activist and does not treat every political question as a test of loyalty; she is more likely to ask what a rule actually does and who bears its costs.",
  "skills": "She is skilled at explaining insurance terms, spotting gaps in a renewal, keeping a client relationship steady during a stressful claim, and organizing complicated information. She is a capable driver and comfortable traveling independently. Her bird identification is strongest for familiar western species and common migrants; she still checks field guides and sometimes confuses similar calls. She is not a particularly handy homeowner and hires help for repairs that require more than basic tools.",
  "behaviors": "On workdays she checks urgent messages early, keeps a short written list of renewal deadlines, and blocks time for client calls so paperwork does not take over the whole day. She tends to eat lunch at her desk during busy weeks, then tries to take a walk after work to clear her head. On weekends she may do groceries and laundry Saturday morning, meet a friend for coffee, and go birding early the next day if the weather looks promising. She keeps a running travel note with dates, possible lodging, and sightings rather than building a minute-by-minute itinerary. She checks work email on vacation more often than she intends, especially during renewals, but can leave it alone once she is outdoors and away from reliable reception.",
  "health": "Grace is generally healthy and able to walk for several hours at a relaxed pace. She spends much of the workday seated and sometimes feels stiff after a week of long calls and paperwork, so ordinary walks are part of her routine. She has no significant health limitation shaping her travel or work choices.",
  "relationships": "Grace is single and has no children. She lives independently and is close to her older sister, who lives in the Portland area; they usually see each other for a meal or family occasion rather than planning every holiday together. Her parents live in the Bay Area, and she calls her mother most weeks and visits when work allows. Nisha is one of her closest friends and her most frequent travel companion. Grace has a wider circle of former classmates and local friends, but she prefers a few dependable relationships to constant social activity.",
  "other_facts": "Grace grew up in the San Jose area and moved to Oregon in her mid-twenties for an entry-level role at an insurance agency. She has stayed because she built a career and a comfortable local routine there, not because she considers Oregon a temporary stop. Her pocket notebook contains bird observations, brief notes about places she may revisit, and the occasional book title she wants to remember."
}

