  "preferences": {
    "travel_and_vacations": {
      "overall": "Scuba diving is the activity that most often gives Grace a longer vacation its shape. She likes the quiet concentration of descending, adjusting to the water, and noticing the reef at its own pace. She is especially pleased by small details—shrimp in a crevice, a camouflaged fish, a change in the light—rather than treating a dive as a hunt for a dramatic sighting. She is an experienced recreational diver, not a technical diver, and is comfortable with ordinary guided boat dives and shore dives. She does not dive alone and will not follow a buddy into a cave or beyond the limits they agreed on. She enjoys a few dives on a good day, but not a schedule that treats every hour underwater as a productivity target.",
      "lodging_and_pace": "She looks for a clean, straightforward guesthouse or small apartment with a place to rinse and store gear, a kitchen or easy breakfast option, and a short route to the water. She would rather spend money on a location that makes diving simple than on a showy room. A liveaboard has not appealed so far: she likes being able to come ashore, read, choose a meal, and take a day off without feeling she is wasting the trip. She prefers a stable base for most of a vacation. Moving between islands or hotels can be worthwhile, but only if the different place offers something she genuinely wants, not merely because it is nearby.",
      "companions_and_planning": "Grace usually goes on dive vacations with one or two friends from a local dive club. Traveling with people who understand buddy checks and rest days is relaxing for her; she is happy to split up on land for an afternoon and meet again for dinner. She can travel alone, but would not make dives without a reliable buddy. She books flights, lodging, and any boat outings ahead, then leaves many meals and land activities open. Her ordinary work leave permits one trip of roughly a week most years, but rain-related sampling deadlines sometimes make her dates less flexible. She also takes occasional local dive weekends along the Southern California coast, which are enjoyable but not a substitute for a warm-water trip.",
      "recent_trip": "In May 2025 Grace spent eight nights on Bonaire with three people from her dive club. The group rented a simple place with a gear-rinse area and a vehicle, and spent several days doing shore dives, with one boat day arranged in advance. Two friends wanted to add Curaçao for a few nights. Grace argued for staying put: she wanted repeated dives at different sites and did not want to give up a full day to another transfer and check-in. She did agree to the boat day, which gave the group a change of pace. One afternoon was windy enough that the water plan was abandoned; she read in the shade and enjoyed having no replacement activity booked. She came home satisfied with the time underwater, though she understood why her friends had wanted a second island. The trip used most of the leave she had set aside for that season and a meaningful part of her travel savings, so she has not immediately booked another international trip.",
      "future_travel": "She is interested in a future trip to the Philippines or Belize, but is not committed to either destination and would choose based on the diving conditions, access, and whether a trusted friend could join. A non-diving city break can appeal too, particularly if it gives her time to read, eat well, and spend a few days without early starts. She visits her parents in Orange County several times a year, but thinks of those visits as family time rather than vacations. She does not need every trip to be a major expedition."
    },
    "reading": "Grace reads for pleasure several times a week, often before bed or with coffee on a weekend morning. She likes literary mysteries, contemporary fiction, and narrative nonfiction, including accessible writing about marine life and coastal environments. She borrows ebooks and print books from the public library, and likes having a paperback for a flight or a quiet hour between dives. She will abandon a book that feels like a chore rather than finishing it out of obligation. Reading is a regular leisure interest in its own right, not just something she does while traveling.",
    "food_and_everyday_leisure": "She likes Korean food from home and also enjoys simple seafood meals and neighborhood restaurants. She cooks a few dependable dinners during the week, often making enough for leftovers, but does not want cooking to become another project after work. At home she may read, watch a mystery series, or catch up with her roommate over tea. She swims laps or does light strength work once or twice a week when her schedule cooperates, mostly because she likes feeling comfortable and capable in the water."
  },
  "beliefs": "Grace identifies as libertarian and prioritizes individual liberty, including personal autonomy and freedom of expression. She is skeptical of government restrictions in both social and economic life and dislikes rules that seem more performative than effective. Her work has made her comfortable with the idea that pollution can impose costs on other people, so she supports clear, evidence-based water-quality limits while remaining critical of regulations she considers needlessly broad or poorly designed. She does not assume that a political label settles every specific policy question, and she does not treat it as a test of friendship.",
  "skills": "She is competent at field sampling, reading water-quality data, coordinating with contractors, writing clear technical reports, and explaining requirements without condescension. She has recreational scuba certifications, is a reliable buddy, and is good at noticing when a dive plan or group pace is becoming uncomfortable. She can identify some common reef life but does not present herself as a marine biologist. She is comfortable driving unfamiliar roads and organizing the essential parts of a trip, though she is not especially interested in optimizing airline points or comparing every minor purchase.",
  "behaviors": "On workdays Grace checks the weather and field schedule before leaving home, keeps practical clothes and sampling supplies in her car, and usually packs lunch rather than deciding at the last minute. She keeps a separate travel savings account and tends to transfer money into it when planning a trip, though she sometimes buys a book or dive accessory before she has fully decided whether she needs it. At home, she and her roommate share basic chores by agreement rather than keeping a strict ledger; Grace is reliable about taking out the trash and less consistent about putting laundry away. Before a dive she checks equipment carefully and listens to the briefing, even when a site seems familiar.",
  "health": "Grace considers herself generally healthy and has no ongoing limitation that shapes her ordinary work or travel. Long field days and multiple dives can leave her tired, so she treats rest, hydration, and not diving when unwell as practical parts of the activity. She is attentive to sun protection and does not treat pushing through discomfort as a point of pride.",
  "relationships": "Grace is single, has no spouse or romantic partner, and has no children. Her roommate is a trusted friend rather than a partner; they enjoy occasional shared meals but do not expect to coordinate every part of their social lives. Her parents live in Orange County, and she sees them regularly without being their primary caregiver. She is close to a younger brother who lives elsewhere in Southern California. Her dive-club friends are an important social circle, though most of their connection is built around diving rather than constant contact.",
  "other_facts": [
    "Grace moved to San Diego in her mid-twenties for environmental consulting work and stayed after finding a public-sector position that better suited the life she wanted.",
    "She joined a local dive club after a coworker invited her to a pool session; the hobby grew gradually through training and local shore dives rather than a single dramatic trip.",
    "She is proud of learning to set a boundary about vacation pace: she will compromise on a shared plan, but no longer agrees to add destinations simply because another stop looks easy on a map."
  ]
}

